FADE IN:

# TEASER

EXT. METROPOLITAN PARKWAY — "BIG E'S CHECK CASHING & PAYDAY LOANS" — DAWN

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 6:12 AM EST

Gray dawn over the cracked Southside strip mall. The yellow sign, "WE SAY YES," still glows, pale against the brightening sky.

A beat-up Toyota pulls into the lot. LATRICE TUGGLE (20s), in a Waffle House uniform with a coffee in hand, gets out, yawning. She's Big E's niece and opens the store every morning at 6:15.

She walks to the glass door. She stops.

The door is ajar. The chain hangs loose. The lights are still on inside.

LATRICE
(uneasy)
Uncle Earl?

She pushes the door open.

INT. BIG E'S CHECK CASHING — CONTINUOUS

The fluorescent lights hum. The radio is still playing quiet storm, very low.

Latrice steps inside. She sees a pair of large legs in work boots on the floor just past the counter.

LATRICE (CONT'D)
Uncle Earl? Uncle Earl, you sleep here again, I swear to God—

She comes around the counter.

Her coffee falls. It hits the linoleum and splashes.

She SCREAMS.

CUT TO:

INT. ELXA & JEREMY'S CRAFTSMAN — VIRGINIA-HIGHLAND — KITCHEN — MORNING

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 6:31 AM EST

A cozy 1920s bungalow kitchen: subway tile, a farmhouse sink, and a wedding photo on the fridge held up by an APD magnet. It's small, warm, and theirs. Elxa chose it because it's twelve miles outside the gates.

Jeremy, in boxers and an old Clark Atlanta T-shirt, is scrambling eggs. Elxa, in a robe, sits at the little kitchen table in front of a stack of case files. It's the first morning all week she's slept in her own bed.

JEREMY
Four and a half hours. That's a new record for this week.

ELXA
(not looking up from the file)
I dreamed about coins.

JEREMY
Romantic.

ELXA
Six crests. Seven crests. Six. Seven. Counting them over and over like sheep.

JEREMY
(sliding eggs onto a plate, setting it in front of her)
Eat something that isn't a vending machine.

She picks up her fork.

On the counter, her cell phone RINGS. Two seconds later, on the kitchen table, Jeremy's pager BUZZES.

They look at each other. Neither moves for a second.

Elxa picks up the phone.

ELXA
Jackson.
(listening)
Where?
(beat)
Metropolitan Parkway.
(beat, her face going very still)
In his hand?

Jeremy turns off the stove.

ELXA (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Nobody touches anything. Nobody talks to anybody. I'm twenty minutes out.

She hangs up. She sits still for one second with the fork still in her hand.

ELXA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Again.

Jeremy is already pulling on his jeans.

SMASH CUT TO:

> MAIN TITLES — "KINGDOM" <

===

# ACT ONE

INT. BIG E'S CHECK CASHING — CRIME SCENE — MORNING

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 7:18 AM EST

The tiny storefront is packed with bodies: crime-scene techs, uniformed officers, and a photographer's flash popping. Outside, yellow tape across the lot. A crowd is gathering behind it in the cold: neighbors, church ladies, and young men with their hoods up and their hands in their pockets.

Latrice sits in the back of a patrol car, wrapped in a blanket, rocking.

Elxa and Jeremy stand behind the counter, gloved, beside the MEDICAL EXAMINER, who crouches over the body of Earl Tuggle. Deputy Chief Hughes stands near the door with his hands in his coat pockets.

MEDICAL EXAMINER
Single gunshot. Close range. Same as Piedmont. Time of death between eleven-thirty and one a.m.

JEREMY
The door was chained.

MEDICAL EXAMINER
He opened it for somebody. Six inches. Look at the right wrist. Bruising. Somebody grabbed it.

ELXA
(quietly)
He knew them. Or he wanted something they had.

The M.E. lifts Big E's left hand. The thick fingers are curled around something. She gently pries them open.

In the dead man's palm: a black ONYX COIN. A crown above a wave.

Jeremy leans down with a small magnifier. He counts silently, lips barely moving.

JEREMY
(barely audible)
Six.

Elxa's jaw tightens.

ELXA
(low, just to him)
Same maker.

JEREMY
(straightening, low)
There's something else.

He turns and points.

On the counter beside the register, under the fluorescent light, where it has sat every day for four years, is a SECOND onyx coin. It's worn and polished, and the crown and wave are hand-cut.

Jeremy leans in with the magnifier. He counts.

JEREMY (CONT'D)
(almost a whisper)
Seven.

Elxa stares at the two coins: one in the dead man's hand and one on his counter, three feet apart.

ELXA
(very quietly)
The real one was already here.

JEREMY
Protection coin. He was paying Blackwater. The neighbors all say it. Four years, nobody touched this store.

ELXA
And somebody killed him with a fake one.
(beat)
Why would Blackwater kill a man they were protecting, with a fake of their own coin, three feet from the real one?

JEREMY
(slowly)
They wouldn't.

They look at each other.

Behind them, Hughes has drifted closer. He's too far to hear the words and close enough to watch their faces.

HUGHES
Chief. Something?

ELXA
(turning, flat)
A dead man, Warren. Tape's twenty feet too close. Push it back.

HUGHES
(beat)
Yes, Chief.

He goes. Elxa turns back to Jeremy and drops her voice even lower.

ELXA
Nobody hears about the second coin. Not until I say so.

JEREMY
Elx, we can't hide physical evidence—

ELXA
I'm not hiding it. I'm logging it. Photographing it. Bagging it. I'm just not reading it out loud at a press conference.

Outside, a commotion at the tape. Car doors slam. Two figures in navy windbreakers with bright yellow letters on the back, "FBI," duck under the tape without asking.

The first: SPECIAL AGENT KATHLEEN "KATE" DOYLE (34), Irish-American from South Boston. She has red hair scraped back into a ponytail, no makeup, and a coffee in one hand. Her eyes are already cataloguing the room before she's through the door.

The second: SPECIAL AGENT DANIEL "DANNY" REYES (29), Puerto Rican from the Bronx. He's handsome, easygoing, with a laptop bag over his shoulder and the relaxed smile of a man who likes spreadsheets more than guns.

DOYLE
(flashing her badge)
Special Agent Kate Doyle. FBI, Atlanta Field Office. This is Special Agent Reyes.
(scanning the room)
Who's in charge?

ELXA
(stepping forward)
I am.

Doyle looks at her. She takes in the uniform, the four stars, and the age.

DOYLE
Chief Jackson.

ELXA
Agent Doyle.

DOYLE
(pleasantly)
Chief. This is a federal case now.

The room goes quiet. The techs stop working.

ELXA
Is it.

DOYLE
Two homicides in five days, both with the signature of an organized criminal enterprise that has been operating across state lines and international borders since at least 2000. The Bureau opened an investigation into the Blackwater Syndicate on November fifteenth. It's called Operation Deep Water. Your department was notified.

ELXA
My department was notified by memo two weeks before I was sworn in. I've read it. It's four pages long and says nothing.

REYES
(helpfully)
It's actually five pages. The fifth one's a map.

Doyle gives him a look. He shrugs.

DOYLE
(to Elxa)
We'll need the physical evidence. Both coins. The ballistics. And the Piedmont file.

Elxa's eyes flick, barely, to Jeremy. Doyle catches it.

DOYLE (CONT'D)
(sharp)
Both coins, Chief. I can count to two. There's one in his hand and one on the counter.

A long, tense beat.

ELXA
(evenly)
The evidence in a homicide in the City of Atlanta is logged, bagged, and stored by the Atlanta Police Department. When the Bureau has a court order, you'll have access to it. Until then, you're welcome to photograph anything you like from behind my tape.

DOYLE
(beat, almost admiring)
You're going to make this hard.

ELXA
I'm going to make it legal.

Doyle looks at Elxa for a long moment. Then she smiles. It isn't friendly, but it isn't hostile either. It's one professional recognizing another.

DOYLE
Fine. We'll do it your way. For now.
(to Reyes)
Photograph everything.

REYES
(already unzipping his laptop bag)
Everything's my favorite thing.

Doyle turns to go. At the door, she stops.

DOYLE
(over her shoulder)
Chief. For the record. I read your file too.
(beat)
Your father defends half the names on my list. Your grandfather's company employs the other half.
(beat)
I'm not accusing you of anything. I'm just telling you what the grand jury's going to ask me.

She walks out into the cold.

Elxa stands very still behind the counter. Jeremy watches her.

JEREMY
(quietly)
You okay?

ELXA
(pulling off her gloves)
She read my file.

JEREMY
She read everybody's file. That's what feds do.

ELXA
(beat)
She's right, though. That's what the grand jury will ask.

She looks at the two coins one more time. Six and seven.

CUT TO:

INT. SAINT'S CAR WASH — THE OFFICE — MORNING

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 7:41 AM EST

The glass office above the bays. The car wash isn't open yet. The bays are dark and silent, the hoses coiled.

Saint stands at the window in yesterday's clothes, phone to his ear. He's listening, not talking. His face goes from confusion to disbelief to something terrible.

SAINT
(into phone, barely)
Say it again.

He listens. He closes his eyes.

SAINT (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Yeah. Yeah. I hear you.

He hangs up. He stands perfectly still.

Then he picks up the folding chair Big E sat in yesterday and HURLS it through the glass wall of the office. It explodes outward and rains down onto the dark car bays below.

He stands in the shattered window, breathing hard.

On his desk, the drawer is still open. Inside is Big E's white envelope from yesterday, uncounted.

Saint looks at it. He picks it up with both hands.

SAINT (CONT'D)
(to the envelope, his voice breaking)
Four years, Unc.

He picks up his phone and dials.

SAINT (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Right Hand.
(beat)
They killed Big E.
(beat, fierce)
He was OURS, Amond. He sat in my chair YESTERDAY. He paid us YESTERDAY. They killed a man with our coin on his counter.
(beat)
I don't care what Sovereign said. I don't care about "cold." I want to know who.
(listening)
...Yeah.
(beat)
Yeah. I'll be there.

He hangs up. He looks down at the broken glass on the floor of the dark car wash.

CUT TO:

INT. MERRIWEATHER & SONS FUNERAL HOME — SILAS'S OFFICE — MORNING

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 8:09 AM EST

Silas stands at his rolltop desk in shirtsleeves and suspenders, not yet in his jacket. His coffee is untouched. Otis stands in the doorway, grim.

OTIS
Earl Tuggle. Last night. Behind his counter. Chained door, open six inches. One shot.
(beat)
And a coin in his hand.

Silas closes his eyes.

SILAS
Earl.

OTIS
You knew him?

SILAS
I lent Earl Tuggle four thousand dollars in 1985 to open that store. He never paid me back a dime. Twenty years.
(beat)
I never asked him for it. He was a good man with a big mouth and bad luck, and he made me laugh at every funeral we ever went to together.

He opens his eyes.

OTIS
Silas. There's something else.

SILAS
Tell me.

OTIS
The streets are saying WE did it.

Silas turns slowly.

SILAS
Say that again.

OTIS
Earl was paying Blackwater protection. Everybody knew it. He kept the coin on his counter like a sign.
(beat)
So now people are saying we did Earl to pay them back for Lamar. That we killed one of theirs for one of ours. And put a coin in his hand to make it look like they turned on him.
(beat)
They're saying it at the barbershops, Silas. My own nephew asked me this morning if it was true.

A long silence. Silas walks slowly to his window and looks across Ralph David Abernathy Boulevard, past the unmarked police car, toward the hand-painted sign two blocks down: DEE'S KITCHEN.

SILAS
(very quietly)
Somebody killed Lamar and made it look like Blackwater did it to me.
(beat)
Then somebody killed Earl and made it look like I did it to them.
(beat)
That's not a war, Otis. That's a man standing between two armies, handing out matches.

OTIS
So who is it?

SILAS
(taking his jacket off the hook, putting it on)
I don't know. But I know who else is asking.

OTIS
You want me to call Dolores?

SILAS
(buttoning his jacket)
No.
(beat)
It's Friday.
(beat)
I'll go myself.

OTIS
(stunned)
To Dee's? Silas, you haven't walked into that restaurant since—

SILAS
Since 1975. When Dolores's husband died and I buried him and she paid me back in cornbread for a year.
(beat)
Tonight. Seven o'clock. Tell her.
(beat)
Tell her I'm hungry.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT TWO

INT. BSI TOWER — 57TH FLOOR — "THE GHOST ROOM" — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 9:14 AM EST

The humming server room. Cold air. Three curved monitors. Keisha sits cross-legged in her chair, typing, with no Froot Loops today.

Around her: Marc, in a black suit with no tie and his face a mask. Amond, arms folded against the wall. Roman, standing by the door like a closed gate.

On Keisha's center monitor: APD crime-scene photos, uploaded thirty minutes ago. The dead man's open hand with its six-crest coin. And the register with its worn seven-crest coin.

KEISHA
APD logged both coins at eight-oh-five. The Chief ordered them photographed and bagged. She didn't list the second coin in the preliminary report that went to the command staff. It's only in the evidence log.

AMOND
She's hiding it from Hughes.

KEISHA
She's hiding it from everybody. Including the FBI.

MARC
(flat)
The FBI.

KEISHA
Special Agent Kathleen Doyle. South Boston. Fourteen years with the Bureau. Brought down two Irish crews and a Russian one in Boston before they moved her south. She opened a file on us in November called Operation Deep Water. She showed up at the scene this morning and told your sister it's a federal case now.

ROMAN
(quietly)
Two bodies in five days. The feds were always going to come.

MARC
(to the screen, very quietly)
That's what he wanted.

AMOND
Who?

MARC
Whoever this is. He didn't just want Silas angry at us. He wanted the FBI in Atlanta by Friday.
(beat)
He's building a case against us with our own coin.

A silence. Keisha types.

KEISHA
Roman. Tell him about the van.

ROMAN
State patrol found a white cargo van burning in a field off Highway 138 in Clayton County at four-ten this morning. Stolen plates, Macon church van. Burned to the frame.
(beat)
Same van from the traffic camera Monday night.

MARC
Burned last night.

ROMAN
A few hours after Big E. He drove it once more, then torched it.

AMOND
So he's done with it.

ROMAN
Or he's got another one.

The door BANGS open. Saint storms in, still in yesterday's clothes, eyes red and wild.

SAINT
Big E was OURS.

MARC
(turning)
Saint—

SAINT
He sat in my office YESTERDAY, Marc. He asked me, man to man, if we did Lamar, and I told him we don't do deacons. I told him that coin on his counter meant nobody touches him. I PROMISED him.
(beat, voice breaking)
And twelve hours later he's dead on his floor with a fake of our coin in his hand and the real one sitting right there. Like a joke. Like somebody's laughing at us.

Nobody speaks. Saint stands in the middle of the room, shaking.

Marc crosses to him slowly. He puts both hands on Saint's shoulders.

MARC
(very quietly)
Law Ten. Every member's family is under Blackwater protection. For life.
(beat)
Big E wasn't a member. But he paid us. He trusted us. His grandbabies are in that picture on his wall.
(beat)
As of this morning, those grandbabies are family. Their school. Their rent. Their Christmas. Everything, until they're grown. Tithe it.

Saint's jaw works. He nods.

MARC (CONT'D)
And Saint.

SAINT
Yeah.

MARC
Whoever did this made one mistake.
(beat)
He left our real coin on the counter. Right next to his fake.
(beat)
That's our proof. That's what we take to Silas tonight. A man who paid us, killed with a counterfeit, three feet from the real thing. We don't kill our own. Everybody in this city knows that. Now we can show it.

AMOND
Silas called Mama Dee an hour ago. He's coming to Dee's tonight. Seven o'clock.

Everyone turns.

ROMAN
He's coming himself?

AMOND
First time in thirty years. He said to tell her he's hungry.

MARC
(beat, something shifting in his face)
Then we feed him.
(to Amond)
You go. With Mama Dee and Saint. You're the Right Hand. You shook his hand in August.

AMOND
And you?

MARC
I'll be in the car.

AMOND
Marc, you can't be anywhere near that restaurant. If Silas's people see your face—

MARC
They'll see a Donohue heir eating fried chicken in a parked car on Ralph David Abernathy. It happens every Friday night in this city.
(beat)
I'm not letting you walk into that room without me close enough to hear it.

Amond looks at him. Then he nods.

KEISHA
(still typing, not looking up)
For the record, I think this is a bad idea.

MARC
Noted.

KEISHA
I'll have the parking lot camera up on my screen.

MARC
Thank you, Ghost.

KEISHA
(still not looking up)
I'm not doing it for you. I'm doing it for Big E's grandbabies.

A beat. Marc looks at her. Then he squeezes Saint's shoulders once more and walks out.

CUT TO:

INT. DONOHUE TOWER — MARTIN'S OFFICE — 86TH FLOOR — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 9:47 AM EST

Martin at his immaculate desk, reading the Wall Street Journal. A television in the corner is muted and tuned to WBKG: Breaking News from the Southside strip mall, yellow tape, flashing blue lights.

His slim black flip phone VIBRATES on the desk. The caller ID reads: INTERNATIONAL.

He lets it ring twice, exactly twice. He closes his office door. He answers.

MARTIN
(into phone)
Good afternoon.
(listening)
Yes. I'm watching it now.
(beat)
Two.
(listening)
No, sir. I was nowhere near it. I was at my desk by seven.
(beat)
And the Bureau arrived this morning. A woman named Doyle. She told the Chief it's a federal case.
(listening, a faint smile)
Exactly as you predicted.

He walks to the window. Two floors up, Victor's office. Across the street, the Smilley sign.

MARTIN (CONT'D)
(into phone)
The Chief is keeping something from her own command staff. I could see it on her face at dinner Wednesday, and I can see it now.
(listening)
I don't know yet. But I'll find out.
(beat)
And the other matter. The Smilley brother.
(listening)
Yes. Wednesday. I have a meeting.
(beat)
Thank you, sir.

He hangs up. He sets the phone on the desk, parallel to the edge.

On the television, WBKG shows a still photo from Big E's family: a big, laughing man holding two small grandchildren on his knees.

Martin looks at it without expression.

Then he unmutes the TV and turns the volume up slightly.

CUT TO:

INT. CITY HALL EAST — APD HEADQUARTERS — PRESS ROOM — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 11:04 AM EST

The cramped press room, fuller than Monday. Fifty reporters and ten cameras. Celeste Vaughn is in the front row in her red coat.

Elxa at the podium in uniform. Beside her, Jeremy, in a borrowed tie, looking like a man who has been handed a live grenade. Behind them, the command staff. Hughes stands at the end of the line.

In the back of the room, arms folded, leaning against the wall: Doyle and Reyes.

ELXA
—in light of two homicides in five days bearing similarities we are not prepared to discuss, the APD Blackwater Task Force, which I announced on December twentieth, moves this morning to a dedicated floor at this headquarters, with twelve detectives and full forensic support.
(beat)
It will be commanded by Detective Jeremy Jackson of the Homicide Unit, reporting directly to me.

A murmur. Pens scribble. A few reporters glance at each other knowingly.

REPORTER #1
Chief. Detective Jackson is your husband.

ELXA
He is.

REPORTER #1
Isn't that nepotism?

ELXA
Detective Jackson has the highest homicide clearance rate in this department. He's an Eagle Scout, a graduate of Clark Atlanta, and the son of a thirty-five-year veteran. He was the best-qualified officer in this building before he married me, and he still is.
(beat)
If anybody finds me a better one, I'll hire him instead and Detective Jackson can take out the trash for a week.

Scattered laughter. Jeremy almost smiles.

REPORTER #2
Chief, is the FBI involved?

ELXA
(beat)
The Atlanta Police Department welcomes the cooperation of our federal partners.

In the back of the room, Doyle raises one eyebrow. Reyes coughs into his fist.

CELESTE
(standing)
Chief. Celeste Vaughn, WBKG. Sources tell us Earl Tuggle was paying protection money to the Blackwater Syndicate. Why would Blackwater kill a man it was protecting?

The room goes quiet. Elxa looks at Celeste for a long moment, then glances down the line at Hughes. Hughes studies his shoes.

ELXA
(evenly)
I don't discuss sources, Ms. Vaughn. Yours or mine.
(beat)
Thank you. That's all.

She steps back from the podium. Cameras flash.

INT. CITY HALL EAST — THE HALLWAY OUTSIDE THE PRESS ROOM — CONTINUOUS

Elxa walks fast. Jeremy hurries to keep up. Doyle falls into step on her other side, matching her stride.

DOYLE
"We welcome the cooperation of our federal partners." That's cute.

ELXA
I thought so.

DOYLE
Your husband?

ELXA
My best detective.

DOYLE
(dry)
And your husband.

ELXA
(stopping, turning)
Agent Doyle. What do you want?

DOYLE
(beat, dropping the edge)
Honestly? I want the two coins, and I want to know what you're not saying about them.
(beat)
I watched your face at the scene this morning, Chief. You looked at that second coin like it was a confession.

Elxa holds her gaze.

ELXA
Get a court order.

DOYLE
(nodding slowly)
I will.
(beat)
But I'll tell you something for free, since we're partners. In fourteen years I've learned one thing about organized crime families.
(beat)
They don't kill their own customers. Somebody's trying very hard to make it look like they do.

Elxa says nothing. But something passes between the two women: a recognition that they are thinking exactly the same thing.

DOYLE (CONT'D)
(turning to go)
Nice press conference, Chief. The trash line was good.

She walks away. Reyes follows, then turns back.

REYES
(to Jeremy, friendly)
Congratulations on the promotion, man. For real.

JEREMY
(surprised)
Thanks.

REYES
(grinning)
Take out the trash anyway. It's good for a marriage.

He goes. Jeremy looks at Elxa.

JEREMY
She's smart.

ELXA
(watching Doyle's red ponytail disappear around the corner)
That's what worries me.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT THREE

INT. DONOHUE TOWER — VICTOR'S OFFICE — 88TH FLOOR — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 12:16 PM EST

Victor at the window in his shirtsleeves, looking tired, with a crick in his neck. Natasha sits on the leather sofa with her BlackBerry and a salad she isn't eating.

NATASHA
You look terrible, Daddy.

VICTOR
I slept on a Chesterfield sofa in my own library, surrounded by Tolstoy, in pajamas Winston pressed with what I can only describe as contempt.

NATASHA
(despite herself, a laugh)
Mama banished you?

VICTOR
For seven nights. "To think about my stupidity among the classics."
(rubbing his neck)
I've been thinking about it among Dostoyevsky. It's not helping.

NATASHA
(sobering)
She knows. About the Mayor.

VICTOR
She suspects.

NATASHA
Mama doesn't suspect, Daddy. Mama sentences.

Victor turns from the window.

VICTOR
The FBI was at a crime scene on Metropolitan Parkway this morning.

NATASHA
I heard. A Special Agent Doyle. She told Elxa it's a federal case.

VICTOR
(sitting heavily behind his desk)
When the Bureau comes to Atlanta for a street crime, Natasha, it isn't for the street. It's for somebody at the top.
(beat)
Call your sister in Washington. Quietly. I want to know who in the Justice Department signed off on Operation Deep Water, and whose names are in the file.

NATASHA
You think we're in it?

VICTOR
I think every family in this city with a skyscraper is in it.
(beat)
And I think your husband's firm has represented more people with cash in their pockets than any other firm in Georgia.

Natasha sets down her BlackBerry.

NATASHA
Martin said the same thing. Tuesday night. In my office.

VICTOR
(beat)
Did he.

NATASHA
He said he was "protecting the family."

VICTOR
(quietly)
Martin has never protected anything in his life that he didn't plan to inherit.

A long pause. Father and daughter look at each other.

NATASHA
Daddy. Is Robert—

VICTOR
Robert is a lawyer, baby. He represents whoever pays him. He always has. That's why I've paid him for twenty years. So that when somebody comes for this family, the best lawyer in the South is already on our side.
(beat)
Whatever else he's doing, he's not doing it with Blackwater.

NATASHA
(beat)
How do you know?

VICTOR
(looking out at the Smilley sign)
Because if he were, somebody would have tried to kill him by now.

Natasha doesn't answer. She picks up her BlackBerry and dials Jasmine.

CUT TO:

INT. SMILLEY TOWER — 69TH FLOOR — DESMOND'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 1:04 PM EST

A corner office of dark wood and leather, cigar-club masculine. Architectural models of luxury condo towers sit under glass. A framed photograph of Desmond shaking hands with a President of the United States.

DESMOND SMILLEY (48), who looks thirty-eight, sits behind the desk with his feet up, flipping a silver lighter open and closed. Across from him sits LUKE SMILLEY (28) in shirtsleeves, tie loosened, restless.

LUKE
—a board meeting. Tuesday. Nine a.m. She sent the agenda this morning. One line: "Smilley Atlanta Real Estate Strategy — E. Smilley."
(beat)
She's taking Estates, Uncle Des. Your division. Your deals. She's walking into your house and rearranging your furniture.

DESMOND
(flipping the lighter)
Let her.

LUKE
LET her?

DESMOND
(swinging his feet down)
Luke. Nephew. Son of my brother.
(the tiniest pause on the word "nephew," which Luke doesn't notice)
When someone reaches too far, you don't slap their hand. You hand them the thing they're reaching for. Then you wait for them to drop it.

LUKE
And if she doesn't drop it?

DESMOND
Everybody drops something eventually.
(beat)
Your sister has a secret. She's had one since Chicago.

LUKE
(frowning)
What secret?

DESMOND
(pleasantly)
If I knew, I'd have used it already.

Luke laughs, but uneasily.

Desmond's desk phone rings. His assistant's voice comes on the speaker.

ASSISTANT (V.O.)
Mr. Smilley, a Mr. Martin Olson on line two. He says it's personal.

Luke's head snaps around.

LUKE
Olson? Isn't that—

DESMOND
(raising a hand for silence, a slow, interested smile)
Donohue. Victor's grandson. The one who isn't the heir.

He picks up the receiver.

DESMOND (CONT'D)
(into phone, warm as honey)
Mr. Olson. What a pleasant surprise.

INTERCUT WITH:

^INT. DONOHUE TOWER — MARTIN'S OFFICE — CONTINUOUS

Martin at his desk, looking across Peachtree toward the silver tower where Desmond sits.

MARTIN
Mr. Smilley. I hope I'm not interrupting.

DESMOND
Not at all. I was just explaining to my nephew the virtue of patience.

MARTIN
(the faintest smile)
A virtue I share.

DESMOND
Is it.
(beat)
To what do I owe the pleasure, Mr. Olson? Our families don't usually do lunch.

MARTIN
Our families don't. But I'm not calling for my family, Mr. Smilley. I'm calling for myself.
(beat)
I think you and I might have the same problem.

A pause. Desmond's smile widens.

DESMOND
And what problem would that be?

MARTIN
A younger cousin who got handed something that should have been ours.

Across the office, Luke goes very still.

DESMOND
(beat)
The Ritz-Carlton. Buckhead. The bar. Wednesday. Nine p.m.

MARTIN
I'll be there.

DESMOND
I'll be the one drinking something expensive.

MARTIN
So will I.

END INTERCUT.

Desmond hangs up. He leans back in his chair and flips the lighter open. A flame.

LUKE
(staring)
You're meeting with a DONOHUE?

DESMOND
(closing the lighter)
I'm meeting with a man who hates his cousin as much as you hate your sister, Luke.
(beat)
That makes him family.

CUT TO:

INT. THE MAJESTIC DINER — PONCE DE LEON AVENUE — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 1:33 PM EST

A legendary 1929 all-night diner: a neon sign, chrome counter stools, cracked vinyl booths, and waitresses who have seen everything.

In a back booth, Jeremy sits across from his father: FORMER CHIEF HAROLD JACKSON (57), who looks forty-eight. He's a big, gray-mustached lion of a man in a Braves windbreaker, with a cup of black coffee and a slice of pie. He retired three weeks ago and doesn't know what to do with his hands.

HAROLD
Commander of the Blackwater Task Force.

JEREMY
Yes, sir.

HAROLD
(shaking his head, proud and worried at once)
Your mother's going to be sick.

JEREMY
She already was. I called her from the car.

HAROLD
What'd she say?

JEREMY
She said, "Tell your wife she better give you a raise."

Harold laughs, a big, warm laugh, then sobers.

HAROLD
Son. Listen to me. I carried a badge for thirty-five years. Six of them as Chief.
(beat)
Blackwater cases end careers.

JEREMY
They're just cases, Pop.

HAROLD
No. They're not.
(leaning in)
Those people have been in this city since 2000, and nobody's ever laid a glove on them. Not us. Not the GBI. Not the feds. You know why?

JEREMY
Why?

HAROLD
Because the people who could lay a glove on them don't want to.
(beat)
Half this city loves them. The other half's scared of them. And the few who aren't either end up doing something else for a living.

JEREMY
Is that what happened to you?

Harold goes still.

HAROLD
What's that supposed to mean?

JEREMY
(carefully)
The first Blackwater case. The one they talk about. Vaughn Harlow. October 2000. Harlow and three of his men disappear, and every one of their apartments has an onyx coin on the kitchen table.
(beat)
You were Chief in 2000, Pop. The file says the case was closed in less than two months. "No viable leads."

Harold takes a long, slow sip of coffee. He sets the cup down.

HAROLD
Harlow was garbage, Jeremy. He drugged teenage girls at parties and sold them. Everybody in this city who mattered wanted him gone.
(beat)
And he's gone.

JEREMY
That's not a reason to close a case.

HAROLD
(sharp)
It's a reason to close THAT case.

The waitress comes by with the coffee pot. Harold smiles at her, all charm again, and waits until she leaves.

HAROLD (CONT'D)
(quieter)
Some cases, son, you work until you find the truth. And some cases, you work until you find out what the truth would cost.
(beat)
Harlow was the second kind.

Jeremy stares at his father.

JEREMY
Who told you to close it?

HAROLD
(picking up his fork, eating his pie)
Nobody told me anything. I was the Chief.

JEREMY
Pop.

HAROLD
(not looking up)
Eat your sandwich, Jeremy. It's getting cold.

Jeremy looks at his father for a long moment: the big hands, the badge he no longer wears, the way he won't meet his son's eyes.

He picks up his sandwich. He doesn't eat it.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT FOUR

INT. KEEL & ASSOCIATES REALTY — BUCKHEAD — HARMONY'S CUBICLE — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 2:31 PM EST

A small, struggling residential brokerage in a strip office on Roswell Road: six cubicles, a coffee maker, and a whiteboard labeled "JANUARY CLOSINGS" with only one name under it, and it isn't Harmony's.

Harmony sits in the smallest cubicle, by the bathroom door, making cold calls from a printed list with a headset on. Pinned to her cubicle wall: a photo of Lyric, a photo of Dee, and a yellow flyer.

HARMONY
(into headset, bright)
—yes, ma'am, a three-bedroom in Stone Mountain with a finished basement and a— Hello? Hello?
(beat)
She hung up.

DONNA KEEL (50s), the brokerage owner, appears at the cubicle opening. She's a tired white woman in a Talbots cardigan who has been in real estate since the Carter administration and is not impressed by anything.

DONNA
Harmony.

HARMONY
(pulling off the headset)
Hi, Donna.

DONNA
Four months. Zero closings. You know that, right?

HARMONY
I have three buyers who are very close—

DONNA
You have three buyers who like looking at houses on Sunday afternoons.
(beat)
I like you. You're sharp, you're hungry, and the clients love you. But I can't keep a desk for someone who doesn't close.

HARMONY
(quietly)
I know.

DONNA
February. You close something by the end of February, or we need to talk about whether this is the right fit.

Harmony nods, swallowing. Donna softens slightly.

DONNA (CONT'D)
And, Harmony. Honey. The flyers.

HARMONY
What about them?

DONNA
Clients see them on your wall. It makes them sad. People don't buy houses when they're sad.

Harmony looks at Dee's face pinned to her wall.

HARMONY
(evenly)
The flyers stay, Donna.

Donna studies her for a moment, then sighs and walks away.

Harmony sits alone in her tiny cubicle. She puts her face in her hands for exactly three seconds.

Her desk phone RINGS. She picks it up, professional again.

HARMONY (CONT'D)
Keel and Associates, this is Harmony.
(beat, eyes widening)
Yes. Yes, this is she.

INTERCUT WITH:

^INT. FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE — DISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OUTER OFFICE — CONTINUOUS

ASSISTANT DA PRESTON at his desk with a file open and a phone to his ear.

PRESTON
Ms. Divine, this is Assistant District Attorney Preston, from District Attorney Cummings's office. The DA asked me to call you personally.
(reading)
Your brother's case, MP-2002-0014, has been formally reassigned to the Cold Case Unit as of this morning. The lead investigator will be Investigator Carla Mims. She has twenty-two years with the GBI. She'll call you Monday afternoon to schedule an interview.
(beat)
The DA asked me to tell you, and I'm reading her note: "Every two weeks. Whether there's news or not. Like I promised."

END INTERCUT.

Harmony can't speak for a second.

HARMONY
(barely)
Thank you. Please tell her thank you.

She hangs up. She sits there for a moment, stunned. Then she pulls the yellow flyer off her cubicle wall and holds it in both hands.

HARMONY (CONT'D)
(whispering to the photo)
Somebody's looking, Dee.

Across the office, Donna glances over and sees the young woman in the smallest cubicle crying and smiling at the same time. She shakes her head and goes back to her coffee.

CUT TO:

INT. FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE — CLERK OF SUPERIOR COURT — RECORDS ROOM — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 3:12 PM EST

A windowless basement records room: rows of gray filing cabinets, a microfiche machine, and a counter with a bored CLERK behind it.

Celeste sits at a public terminal in her red coat, with three legal pads, a highlighter, and a stack of printouts. She's been here for two hours. She's scrolling through the court's computerized docket, case after case.

CLERK
(from the counter)
Ma'am, we close at four-thirty.

CELESTE
(not looking up)
I know. I'll be out by four twenty-nine.

She scrolls. She writes. She highlights.

!INSERT — HER LEGAL PAD, in fast handwriting: "Harlow ring — arrests 2000 — 7 defendants. Counsel of record: Bullock & Assoc. — 3 of 7. Payday/check cashing — gambling cases 2001–04 — Bullock & Assoc. — 11 of 19. Southside 'fires' summer 04 — insurance claims — civil — Bullock & Assoc. rep. 4 plaintiffs?"

She stops. She sits back. She looks at the page.

She flips back through her pad to the first page, where one word is underlined twice: "COURTHOUSE."

CELESTE
(to herself, very quietly)
Oh, Martin.

She pulls out her RAZR, opens it, and scrolls to "MARC <3." Her thumb hovers.

She closes the phone without calling.

She tears the page off the legal pad, folds it carefully, and slides it into her coat's inside pocket.

CELESTE (CONT'D)
(to the clerk, gathering her things)
Four twenty-eight. See? A minute early.

CLERK
(unimpressed)
Congratulations.

CUT TO:

INT. THE OLSON ESTATE — THE CATERING KITCHEN — DAY

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 4:06 PM EST

A second, industrial kitchen in the Olson mansion, built for parties and now converted into a makeshift LAB: stainless counters covered in beakers, a digital scale, a double boiler, labeled glass jars, and bags of shea butter.

Grace and Mallory, in aprons and hairnets, stand over the green leather FORMULARY, which is propped open on a cookbook stand under a protective plastic sheet.

GRACE
(reading the faded ink)
"Rose Balm Number Seven. Four parts shea from the Kumasi woman. Two parts beeswax from Hollis, Thomasville. One part rose attar. And vetiver—"
(squinting)
"—one drop. ONE. Not three. One. And Alexander can hush."

MALLORY
(laughing, measuring)
She wrote "Alexander can hush" in her formula book?

GRACE
She wrote it in the MARGIN. Look. There's a whole argument in the margins. He wrote "too much lady" in pencil, and she wrote "hush" in ink right over it.

They lean in together, delighted, reading seventy-year-old marital bickering in the margins of a chemistry book.

MALLORY
(carefully adding one single drop of vetiver)
One.

She stirs the double boiler. Grace leans in and sniffs.

GRACE
(eyes closing)
Oh.
(beat)
That's it. That's the jar Nana Ruby used. That's my knuckles in January.

MALLORY
(smelling it, stunned)
It's different. It's so much better than the drugstore version.

GRACE
That's because the drugstore version is a copy.

Mallory turns off the burner. She wipes her hands on her apron.

MALLORY
Speaking of copies. Something weird happened this morning.

GRACE
Weird how?

MALLORY
I called the shea cooperative in Ghana. The one Great-Grandma wrote down, "the Kumasi women." They're still in business. Third generation. And the lady said...
(beat)
She said someone else called them a few hours before me, asking about "the Toussaint shea." An American woman. From a company. Asking if they'd ever supplied Donohue.

GRACE
(frowning)
Who?

MALLORY
She didn't get a name. She said the woman had a very "fancy" voice and wanted to know who else was buying.

Grace sets down her spoon.

GRACE
This morning? We only GOT the book yesterday.

MALLORY
I know.

They look at each other. Then, slowly, both look down at the green leather book on its stand.

GRACE
(quietly)
Somebody knew before we did.

MALLORY
Or somebody's been waiting a long time.

Grace closes the formulary gently and slides it into a zippered leather case.

GRACE
This book doesn't leave our sight.

MALLORY
(nodding)
Not for a second.

CUT TO:

INT. RAVENHURST — LUCIUS'S STUDY — DUSK

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 5:14 PM EST

A dark, masculine study: leather, first editions, a portrait of Cornelius Smilley in his Pullman porter's uniform, proud and unsmiling, and a humidor. The last light fades outside.

Lucius sits behind his desk with a brandy. Esther stands in front of it, holding a thin leather portfolio.

LUCIUS
Harmony Divine.

ESTHER
Yes.

LUCIUS
As exclusive broker for every Smilley acquisition in Atlanta.

ESTHER
Yes.

LUCIUS
A twenty-two-year-old from Cascade Heights with four months in the business, zero closings, and a Honda Civic that sounds like a blender.

ESTHER
(beat)
How do you know about the Civic?

LUCIUS
Crowe tells me everything, darling. That's what I pay him for.

Esther sets the portfolio on his desk.

ESTHER
Daddy. Every broker in Buckhead has a relationship with the Donohues. Every single one. They've sold Belmont Crest houses, they've golfed at the Club, and they've taken DGH commissions for thirty years.
(beat)
If I hire any of them, the Donohues will know what we're buying before the ink dries.

LUCIUS
And your friend?

ESTHER
My friend has never taken a dime from anybody in that family. She'd rather die than owe them.
(beat)
She's the only broker in Atlanta I know for certain can't be bought by Victor Donohue.

Lucius studies his daughter.

LUCIUS
She has a child with a Belmont Crest boy. The dentist's son. The one who works for the Donohue heir.

ESTHER
(evenly)
She co-parents through lawyers. They barely speak.

LUCIUS
(beat)
And the heir is the child's godfather.

Esther doesn't answer immediately.

ESTHER
(carefully)
Harmony is loyal to me, Daddy. Not to them.

LUCIUS
(swirling his brandy)
Everybody's loyal until they're offered the right thing.
(beat)
Fine. Tuesday. Present it to the board. Luke will object and Delphine will sneer, and I'll back you, because you're usually right.
(beat)
But if your friend so much as has lunch with Victor Donohue's grandson...

ESTHER
She won't.

LUCIUS
(beat)
Make sure.

He reaches for the portfolio. As he does, his right hand TREMBLES badly. The portfolio slides off the edge of the desk and hits the carpet, spilling papers.

A frozen moment. Father and daughter look at the papers on the floor. Neither moves.

Then Esther kneels and gathers them calmly, as if nothing has happened. She stacks them neatly and sets the portfolio back on the desk, square in front of him.

ESTHER
(quietly)
Tuesday, Daddy.

LUCIUS
(not meeting her eyes)
Tuesday.

She goes. At the door she pauses and looks back. He's sitting very still, his right hand pressed flat against the desk as if holding it down.

Above him, Cornelius Smilley looks down from his portrait, unsmiling.

CUT TO:

INT. WBKG CHANNEL 8 — NEWSROOM — CELESTE'S DESK — DUSK

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 5:41 PM EST

A busy local newsroom: cubicles, police scanners crackling, a wall of monitors showing every Atlanta station, and producers yelling about the six o'clock.

Celeste sits at her cluttered desk with the folded legal-pad page flattened in front of her. She dials a number from a business card.

CELESTE
(into phone, low)
It's Celeste Vaughn.

INTERCUT WITH:

^INT. DONOHUE TOWER — MARTIN'S OFFICE — CONTINUOUS

Martin at his desk, the city going dark outside his window.

MARTIN
Miss Vaughn. You shouldn't call this number.

CELESTE
You gave it to me.

MARTIN
I gave you a card. Cards are for emergencies.

CELESTE
(looking at the page)
Three of seven.

A pause on Martin's end.

MARTIN
I'm sorry?

CELESTE
The Harlow ring. Seven defendants in 2000. Three of them were represented by Bullock and Associates. Eleven of nineteen check-cashing gambling cases since 2001. And four of the plaintiffs in the insurance suits after last summer's Southside fires.
(beat)
That's a lot of counting, Martin.

MARTIN
(evenly)
Defense attorneys represent defendants. That's the job.

CELESTE
Then why did you send me to count?

MARTIN
(beat)
I didn't send you anywhere, Miss Vaughn. I ate a biscuit.

CELESTE
(a short laugh)
Right.
(beat, serious)
If I run this, it's my boyfriend's father.

MARTIN
If you run it, it's the truth. Whose father it is doesn't change the arithmetic.
(beat)
But you don't run it yet. Not with three and eleven and four. You run it when you have one more number.

CELESTE
What number?

MARTIN
(pleasantly)
Good night, Miss Vaughn.

He hangs up.

END INTERCUT.

Celeste looks at the dead phone. She looks at the page. Then she looks up at the wall of monitors, where her own station is running footage of Big E's strip mall behind yellow tape.

She folds the page and puts it back in her pocket.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT FIVE

INT. BELLE RIVE — THE MASTER SUITE — DUSK

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 6:32 PM EST

The silk-walled bedroom. Robert sits on the edge of the bed, pulling off his cufflinks after a long week. Natasha stands at her vanity, removing her earrings.

NATASHA
(pleasantly, not turning)
Robert. Monday night. You came home at seven in a different shirt.

ROBERT
(beat)
Did I?

NATASHA
Ruby noticed. You told her you spilled decaf.

ROBERT
I did spill decaf.

NATASHA
(turning to face him)
Where did the first shirt go?

A long pause. The question lands exactly where Nana Ruby aimed it.

ROBERT
(steady)
Dry cleaner. On the way home. Peachtree Battle.

NATASHA
The dry cleaner on Peachtree Battle closes at six.

ROBERT
(beat)
Then I left it in the car.

NATASHA
(beat)
Then I'll look in the car.

ROBERT
(standing, gently)
Baby. It's a shirt.

NATASHA
(evenly)
It's a question, Robert. I'm allowed to ask my husband a question in my own bedroom.

He crosses to her. He puts his hands on her shoulders and looks at her in the mirror.

ROBERT
Twenty-three years, Natasha.

NATASHA
I know how long it's been.

ROBERT
Then you know me.

NATASHA
(looking at him in the mirror, very quietly)
That's what worries me.

She moves out from under his hands and walks into the dressing room. The door closes, not hard. Politely.

Robert stands alone in front of the mirror.

He looks at his reflection for a long moment.

CUT TO:

INT. DEE'S KITCHEN — WEST END — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 7:00 PM EST

Friday dinner rush. Every red booth is full. Families, church groups, young couples on dates. The smell of fried chicken and cornbread. The gospel radio on the counter plays Mahalia Jackson.

Behind the counter, Mama Dee in her white apron plates up smothered pork chops with brisk grace. TANISHA, the young waitress, balances four plates on one arm.

The bell over the door JINGLES.

Mama Dee doesn't look up at first. Then she notices the silence spreading from the front of the restaurant like a ripple across a pond.

Every conversation stops. Forks pause halfway to mouths. A child stops mid-sentence.

In the doorway stands Silas Merriweather. Black cashmere overcoat, black fedora, silver beard, a gold watch chain across his vest. Otis stands one step behind him, his hands visible and empty.

The whole restaurant knows who he is. The whole restaurant knows who owns this one.

Silas takes off his fedora, slowly, the way a man does when he walks into a church.

He looks across the room at Mama Dee.

She sets down the plate in her hands. She wipes her hands on her apron. She looks back at him.

MAMA DEE
(clear, calm, carrying across the silent room)
Friday, Silas.

SILAS
Friday, Dolores.

A long, electric beat. Thirty years in a look.

MAMA DEE
You hungry?

SILAS
(a slight nod)
I told Otis to tell you.

MAMA DEE
(turning to the kitchen)
Tanisha. Two smothered chicken, collards, mac and cheese, candied yams. And a whole sweet-potato pie. Back room.
(to the silent restaurant, brightly)
Y'all go on and eat. It's just an old friend.

The restaurant slowly, nervously resumes. Silas walks through the room with his fedora in his hand. People lean back from their tables as he passes. An elderly woman in a church hat reaches out and touches his sleeve.

ELDERLY WOMAN
(softly)
You buried my husband, Mr. Merriweather. You did right by him.

SILAS
(stopping, gently)
He was a good man, Miss Cora. He deserved the silk lining.

He moves on. Mama Dee holds open the swinging kitchen door. He passes through. Otis follows.

EXT. RALPH DAVID ABERNATHY BOULEVARD — ACROSS FROM DEE'S KITCHEN — CONTINUOUS

A dark silver Aston Martin is parked at the curb under a broken streetlight. Its engine is off.

Inside, in the driver's seat, in a black hoodie: Marc. A foil container of fried chicken sits on the passenger seat, untouched. A cell phone rests on the dashboard, on speaker, connected to a line that's open and silent.

He watches the restaurant's bright windows.

Through them, he saw Silas walk in. He saw the room go still.

He picks up a drumstick. He doesn't eat it.

INT. DEE'S KITCHEN — THE BACK ROOM — CONTINUOUS

A small private dining room behind the kitchen, used for church repasts and family reunions: one long table with a red-checked cloth, folding chairs, and a framed photo of Dolores and her late husband on their wedding day in 1970.

At the table: Mama Dee at one end. Silas at the other. Amond on Mama Dee's right. Saint on her left, jaw tight and eyes red. Otis stands by the door.

In Amond's breast pocket, unseen, his phone is on. The line is open.

Tanisha brings the food and leaves quickly, closing the door behind her.

Nobody eats.

SILAS
(to Amond)
Young man. The last time I sat across a table from you in this building, you told me I could keep my funeral homes and my pride.

AMOND
I remember.

SILAS
Five months later, two men are dead. One of mine and one of yours.
(beat)
And the streets are saying we killed each other's.

AMOND
Yes, sir.

SILAS
So. It's Friday. Dolores promised me proof.

Amond reaches into his jacket, the side without the phone, and takes out a small velvet pouch and a manila envelope. He sets them in the center of the table.

AMOND
Mr. Merriweather. Open the pouch.

Silas looks at him, then picks up the pouch and tips it into his palm.

A polished BLACK ONYX COIN. Crown. Wave. Hand-cut, worn smooth, heavy.

AMOND (CONT'D)
That's ours. It's one of the first thousand. Mama Dee's had it since 2000.
(beat)
Count the crests on the wave.

Silas looks at him, then at the coin. He takes a pair of reading glasses from his vest pocket and puts them on. He holds the coin up to the light.

SILAS
(slowly)
One. Two. Three. Four. Five. Six.
(beat)
Seven.

AMOND
Now open the envelope.

Silas sets the coin down and opens the envelope. Inside are two photographs, eight-by-ten and glossy, taken this morning at Big E's by a police photographer, though Silas doesn't ask how Amond has them.

The first: Big E's dead hand, open, a coin in the palm.
The second: the coin on the counter by the register.

Silas holds the first photo close. He counts.

SILAS
(very quietly)
Six.

He looks at the second photo. He counts.

SILAS (CONT'D)
Seven.

He sets both photographs down side by side on the red-checked tablecloth. He looks at them for a long time.

AMOND
The seven on the counter was Big E's protection coin. He'd had it four years. He paid us every first Thursday. Saint collected it himself. Yesterday.

SAINT
(voice rough)
He sat in my office at three o'clock yesterday afternoon, Mr. Merriweather. He asked me if we did Lamar. I told him no.
(beat)
Twelve hours later he's on his floor with a fake in his hand and the real one three feet away.

AMOND
We don't kill men who pay us. We don't kill deacons. And we don't make coins with six crests.
(beat)
Somebody is making them for us.

A long silence. Silas takes off his reading glasses and folds them.

SILAS
And I didn't kill Earl Tuggle.
(beat)
I lent that man four thousand dollars in 1985 to open that store. He never paid me back and I never asked. I've been to eleven funerals with Earl Tuggle, and he made me laugh at every one.
(beat)
But my own nephew asked me this morning if I did it.

MAMA DEE
(quietly)
Then somebody's lying to both of us, Silas.

SILAS
(nodding slowly)
Somebody killed my man and made it look like you. Then killed yours and made it look like me.
(beat)
That's not a war. That's a match in a room full of gasoline.

He looks down the table at Mama Dee.

SILAS (CONT'D)
Who, Dolores?

MAMA DEE
(beat)
We don't know yet. But he's got money. He's got a laser machine that cuts onyx. He's got a tall white man in a van who moves like a soldier.
(beat)
And he's got patience, Silas. He's been planning this since before Christmas.

SILAS
A tall white man.

AMOND
In a white van. Stolen plates from a church in Macon. Burned it in Clayton County at four this morning.

Silas looks at Otis. Otis shakes his head: nobody we know.

Silas picks up the seven-crest coin again and turns it over in his fingers.

SILAS
My daddy told me something when I was a boy. He said the most dangerous man in any room is the one who isn't in it.
(beat)
Somebody's standing outside this room, Dolores, pouring gasoline under the door.

He sets the coin down in front of Mama Dee. He slides it back to her.

SILAS (CONT'D)
The truce holds.

A long exhale around the table. Saint closes his eyes.

SILAS (CONT'D)
Lamar's funeral is tomorrow at eleven. Greater Cascade. Four hundred people.
(beat)
I want your people outside that church. Next to mine. Side by side. Let the whole Southside see Merriweather and Blackwater standing on the same sidewalk.

SAINT
(stunned)
Together?

SILAS
Together, young man. That's the only thing that'll stop the talking.
(beat)
And when we find the man who did this...

AMOND
We share.

SILAS
(a slow, grim smile)
We share.

He holds out his hand across the table to Amond. Amond stands and shakes it.

Then Silas turns to Mama Dee and holds out his hand to her too. She looks at it for a moment, then takes it in both of hers.

MAMA DEE
You still owe me for 1975.

SILAS
(squeezing her hands)
I paid you in cornbread for a year.

MAMA DEE
That was MY cornbread, Silas.

For the first time all night, Silas Merriweather laughs. It's a deep, surprised sound.

Saint laughs too, wiping his eyes. Even Otis smiles.

SILAS
(sitting back down, pulling his plate toward him)
Now. I'm going to eat this smothered chicken. And then I'm going to eat that entire pie. And nobody at this table is going to say one word to me until I'm done.

MAMA DEE
(pushing the pie toward him)
Eat, Silas.

He eats.

EXT. RALPH DAVID ABERNATHY BOULEVARD — THE ASTON MARTIN — CONTINUOUS

On the dashboard, through the open line on the phone, faintly: laughter. Silverware. Mama Dee's voice saying something about cornbread.

Marc sits in the dark car. He listened to all of it.

He closes his eyes. He exhales. For the first time all day, his shoulders drop an inch.

He picks up the drumstick and takes a bite.

MARC
(to himself, mouth full)
Damn, that woman can cook.

LATER.

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 8:22 PM EST

Silas comes out of Dee's Kitchen with Otis. He's full and a little lighter on his feet. He puts his fedora on and looks up and down the boulevard.

His eyes land on the silver Aston Martin across the street under the broken light. Inside, a young man in a hoodie is eating fried chicken out of a foil container.

Silas squints. He knows that face. Every Black man of means in Atlanta knows that face.

He watches the young man eat. The young man doesn't look up.

SILAS
(to Otis, quietly, puzzled)
Victor Donohue's grandson is eating Dolores's chicken in a sports car on Ralph David Abernathy Boulevard on a Friday night.

OTIS
(glancing over)
Rich kids like soul food.

SILAS
(beat)
Mm-hm.

He watches a moment longer. Then he turns and walks toward his black Lincoln.

SILAS (CONT'D)
(not looking back)
Strange city, Otis.

OTIS
(opening the car door)
Yes, sir.

SILAS
(getting in)
Strange city.

The Lincoln pulls away.

In the Aston Martin, Marc watches it go in the rearview mirror. Then he wipes his fingers on a napkin.

Amond comes out of the restaurant, crosses the street, and gets into the passenger seat.

AMOND
Truce holds.

MARC
I heard.

AMOND
(looking at the foil container)
You ate all the chicken.

MARC
I saved you a wing.

AMOND
You saved me a NECK.

MARC
(starting the car)
It's the best part.
(beat, serious)
Now we hunt.

AMOND
(beat)
Now we hunt.

The Aston Martin pulls out onto the boulevard and disappears into the night.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT SIX

INT. FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE — DISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OFFICE — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 8:48 PM EST

The office is dark except for the desk lamp and the blue glow of a computer monitor. The building is empty. Friday night.

Arianna sits at her desk in her shirtsleeves with her jacket over the chair and her heels kicked off under the desk. A takeout container of pad thai sits untouched.

She's been working. Now she isn't.

On the monitor: a LiveJournal page with a yellow-and-white layout.

!INSERT — THE SCREEN: "FIND DEE — finddee.livejournal.com — Friday, January 7th, 2005 — 6:14 pm. Current mood: hopeful. Current music: Donny Hathaway, 'A Song for You.'"

Arianna reads. We hear the words in HARMONY'S VOICE, over the silent office.

HARMONY (V.O.)
"The DA's office called me at work today. Personally. Dee's case has an investigator now. Her name is Carla Mims, and she has twenty-two years with the GBI, and she's calling me Monday."
(beat)
"The DA sent a note. It said, 'Every two weeks. Whether there's news or not. Like I promised.'"

Arianna's hand goes to her mouth.

HARMONY (V.O.) (CONT'D)
"I've been writing on this page every week for three years. I've written to the police and the mayor and the newspapers and a lady on the radio. Nobody ever wrote back."
(beat)
"Arianna Cummings wrote back."

Arianna's eyes fill.

HARMONY (V.O.) (CONT'D)
"She's twenty-two, like me. She broke a pen in her hand because she was so upset for us. Daddy said Dee used to do that when he was trying not to cry."
(beat)
"For the first time in three years, I believe somebody."
(beat)
"Thank you, Arianna. From me, and Mama, and Daddy, and Lyric."
(beat)
"And from Dee. Wherever he is."

The screen shows a photo: the grinning young barber holding his clippers.

Arianna stares at it.

Then she breaks.

It isn't the controlled, silent tears of last night in the gazebo. Something gives way all at once. She folds forward over the desk and presses her face into her arms, and her whole body shakes with sobs she has held back for three years. They're ugly, choking, and animal.

The monitor glows. The grinning barber looks out over her head.

After a long moment she sits up, gasping. Mascara runs down her face. She grabs her car keys off the desk, doesn't bother with her coat or her shoes, and runs out barefoot.

The door swings shut behind her. The monitor stays on, glowing over the empty desk.

CUT TO:

INT. VELVET NIGHTCLUB — BUCKHEAD — THE DJ BOOTH — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 9:06 PM EST

Buckhead's hottest club, before the crowd arrives: velvet banquettes, a mirrored bar, purple lights, and a huge empty dance floor. Bartenders slice limes. A bouncer eats a sandwich by the rope.

In the elevated DJ booth, flipping through a crate of vinyl, is QUENTIN SMILLEY (21). He's lanky and handsome, in a vintage Members Only jacket and a fitted cap, with headphones around his neck. He's clowning and secretly nervous, and he's much less stupid than he pretends to be.

A harried CLUB MANAGER in a black suit climbs the booth stairs.

CLUB MANAGER
You're the Chicago kid.

QUENTIN
I'm the Chicago MAN. Q. Nice to meet you.

CLUB MANAGER
You got a two-hour set. Ten to midnight. Warm-up for the headliner. Don't play anything anybody knows.

QUENTIN
(grinning)
So, nothing good?

CLUB MANAGER
Nothing anybody knows. People don't dance to warm-up, they drink to it. Keep 'em thirsty.

QUENTIN
(saluting)
Thirsty. Got it.
(beat, looking around)
Nice place. Who owns it?

CLUB MANAGER
(lowering his voice)
Officially? A bunch of LLCs. Unofficially, it's Alvin Donohue's. Him and some partners.

QUENTIN
(pausing, a record in his hand)
Donohue.

CLUB MANAGER
Victor's son. The one who parties.
(beat)
Don't worry, he's in Miami. He's been in Miami for a year. He sends champagne and gets the bills.

QUENTIN
(slowly, a sly smile)
So I'm spinning in a Donohue club.

CLUB MANAGER
Is that a problem?

QUENTIN
(sliding the record out of its sleeve)
Man, my whole family would have a stroke.
(beat)
That's not a problem. That's a bonus.

The manager shakes his head and climbs down.

Quentin drops the needle. A deep, warm Chicago house groove fills the empty club. He closes his eyes and nods along, alone in the booth.

His flip phone buzzes. The screen reads "DAD."

He looks at it. He lets it go to voicemail.

He turns the music up.

CUT TO:

INT. DONOHUE MANOR — THE LIBRARY — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 9:31 PM EST

The twenty-thousand-book library. The fire burns low. The quilt and pillow are on the Chesterfield.

Victor sits in a leather armchair in his pressed pajamas and a silk robe, reading *Anna Karenina* with an expression of mild suffering.

The door opens. Joan stands there in her robe, holding a tray: a pot of chamomile tea and one cup.

Victor sets the book down, surprised.

JOAN
(not coming in yet)
Don't get excited.

VICTOR
I'm not excited.

JOAN
Night two of seven.

VICTOR
I'm aware of the arithmetic, Joan.

She comes in and sets the tray on the side table beside him. She pours one cup of tea. She doesn't pour one for herself.

JOAN
The FBI was at a crime scene this morning. Special Agent Kathleen Doyle. She told our granddaughter it's a federal case.

VICTOR
I heard.

JOAN
Natasha called Jasmine. Jasmine called a friend at Justice. The file is called Operation Deep Water. It's been open since November.
(beat)
It has our family's name in it, Victor. Three times.

VICTOR
(beat)
Whose names?

JOAN
Robert's firm. Marc's company. And Donohue Shipping.

Victor sets down his cup very slowly.

VICTOR
Shipping.

JOAN
Something about the Port of Savannah. Jasmine's friend didn't know more.

Victor says nothing. The word "Savannah" hangs in the room.

JOAN (CONT'D)
(watching him)
I didn't come in here to forgive you.

VICTOR
I know.

JOAN
I came in here because whatever you did for Elxa, you did because you love her. I know that. I've always known that about you.
(beat)
But the FBI doesn't care why you did things, Victor. It only cares that you did them.

VICTOR
(quietly)
I know that too.

JOAN
Then stop doing them.

She turns to go. At the door, she stops.

JOAN (CONT'D)
(without turning)
Drink your tea. It's the good chamomile. From the Dowager House.

VICTOR
(beat)
Mother sent it?

JOAN
(beat)
I asked her for it.

She leaves. The door closes softly.

Victor looks at the single cup of tea. He picks it up with both hands and drinks.

CUT TO:

INT. CITY HALL EAST — APD — BLACKWATER TASK FORCE FLOOR — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 9:52 PM EST

A half-unpacked floor of cubicles. Whiteboards covered in photos: Lamar Whitlock, Earl Tuggle, maps with pins, a traffic-cam still of a white van. Boxes everywhere. Detectives have gone home.

Elxa sits on the edge of a desk in her uniform with her jacket off, a phone to her ear. Jeremy writes on a whiteboard behind her.

!INSERT — THE WHITEBOARD: "COIN #1 (PARK) — 6 CRESTS — FAKE. COIN #2 (BIG E HAND) — 6 — FAKE. COIN #3 (BIG E COUNTER) — 7 — REAL?? → WHO BENEFITS?"

ELXA
(into phone)
Arianna, it's Elxa. It's almost ten, sorry.
(beat)
I wanted you to hear this from me before Monday. Off the record for now.
(beat)
There were two coins at Big E's. The one in his hand was a fake. Six crests, same as the park. And the one on his counter was real. Seven. He'd had it for years.
(beat)
Blackwater didn't kill these men, Ari. Somebody's framing them. Somebody who wants the feds here, and wants the Southside burning.
(beat)
Call me back. I want your office on this from the start. The moment I have a name.

She hangs up. She stares at the whiteboard.

JEREMY
(capping his marker)
She didn't pick up?

ELXA
Voicemail.
(beat)
Arianna always picks up.

JEREMY
It's Friday night. Maybe she's got a life.

ELXA
(beat)
Arianna doesn't have a life. She has a docket.

She looks at her phone for a moment. Then at the whiteboard: "WHO BENEFITS?"

ELXA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Who benefits, Jeremy? From making Blackwater look like murderers?

JEREMY
Anybody who wants them gone.

ELXA
(beat)
Or anybody who wants what they have.

She picks up the marker and writes under "WHO BENEFITS?" a single word: "SUCCESSION?"

Then she looks at it and erases it with her palm.

ELXA (CONT'D)
I'm tired. Let's go home.

CUT TO:

EXT. A SMALL BRICK HOUSE — SOUTHWEST ATLANTA — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 10:06 PM EST

A modest two-bedroom ranch on a quiet street off Campbellton Road. A porch light. A tricycle in the yard. A church wreath still hanging on the door from Christmas.

Mama Dee, in her church coat and hat, climbs the porch steps carrying two heavy grocery bags. Saint follows with two more, and a large white envelope tucked under his arm.

She rings the bell.

The door opens a crack on the chain. A red-eyed young woman in a bathrobe looks out: LATRICE TUGGLE, Big E's niece, who found him this morning. Behind her, the sound of a TV, and two small children, four and six, peeking around her legs in their pajamas.

LATRICE
(hoarse)
Can I help you?

MAMA DEE
(gently)
Baby, I'm Dolores Whitfield. From Dee's Kitchen. Your uncle Earl used to come in every Sunday after church and eat two orders of oxtails and complain they were too small.

LATRICE
(a broken half-laugh)
That's him.

MAMA DEE
I brought groceries. And this young man brought something else.

Saint steps forward and holds out the envelope with both hands.

SAINT
(quietly)
For the babies. From people who cared about your uncle.

Latrice looks at the envelope without taking it.

LATRICE
What is it?

SAINT
Rent. For a year. And their school. And Christmas. Every year. Until they're grown.

Latrice stares at him. She looks at the envelope. She looks at the two children at her knees.

Then she looks at Saint, and realizes who he is. Who he works for.

LATRICE
(barely a whisper)
Y'all didn't do it. Did you?

SAINT
(his voice breaking, just slightly)
No, ma'am.
(beat)
We'd have died for him.

A long moment. Latrice unhooks the chain. She takes the envelope with trembling hands.

The four-year-old, a little boy in dinosaur pajamas, looks up at Mama Dee.

LITTLE BOY
Are you a grandma?

MAMA DEE
(crouching down, smiling through tears)
I'm everybody's grandma, sugar. Now help me carry these groceries before my knees give out.

The little boy grabs a bag of bread and marches it inside importantly. Mama Dee follows.

Saint stands on the porch alone for a moment, looking at the tricycle in the yard.

Then he follows them in.

CUT TO:

INT. THE BULLOCK PAVILION — LIVING ROOM — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 7, 2005 — 10:31 PM EST

The glass house over the river. Marc, back in the black hoodie, sits cross-legged on the floor in front of the fireplace with his laptop open. Alexander's pocket watch lies on the coffee table, ticking.

On the laptop screen: Keisha's latest report. The list of eleven onyx-cutting shops around the world. Four are crossed off.

His RAZR RINGS. He looks at it.

!INSERT — CALLER ID: "ARI"

He answers.

MARC
Hey. Did you eat the ginger snap—

INTERCUT WITH:

INT. ARIANNA'S BMW — PARKED — NORTHSIDE DRIVE NW — NIGHT

A dark, winding stretch of Northside Drive in the wooded hills of northwest Atlanta. No streetlights. Bare trees crowd the road on both sides.

Arianna's silver BMW sits pulled onto the narrow shoulder, engine running, headlights on. The beams point down the road at a sharp CURVE, where a battered metal guardrail runs along the edge of a wooded ravine.

Arianna sits behind the wheel, barefoot, in her shirtsleeves, mascara streaked down her face, clutching the phone with both hands. She's sobbing so hard she can barely get the words out.

ARIANNA
(hysterical)
She asked ME, Marc.

MARC
(instantly on his feet)
Ari—

ARIANNA
She asked ME to find him, Marc. She asked ME.

MARC
Ari, breathe—

ARIANNA
She wrote about me. On the internet. She said I wrote back. She said I'm the first person in three years she believes. She THANKED me, Marc. From her mother and her father and Lyric and—
(a terrible sob)
—and from DEE. "Wherever he is."

In the Pavilion, Marc is already moving. He grabs his keys off the counter and his coat off the hook.

MARC
Where are you?

ARIANNA
(staring out the windshield at the curve in the headlights)
Where do you think I am?

Marc stops dead in the middle of his living room with his hand on the door.

MARC
(very quietly)
Ari.

ARIANNA
Northside Drive.
(beat)
The curve. By the guardrail.
(beat)
I'm looking at it, Marc. I'm looking right at it.

Marc closes his eyes.

MARC
(low, steady, the voice he uses with frightened children)
Listen to me. Lock your doors. Turn off the engine. Don't get out of the car.
(beat)
Don't get out of the car, Ari. Do you hear me?

ARIANNA
(barely)
I hear you.

MARC
I'm coming. Nine minutes. Stay on the phone.

He's out the door and running. The glass door swings shut behind him. On the coffee table, Alexander's pocket watch keeps ticking in the empty house.

In the BMW, Arianna presses the phone to her ear. She doesn't turn off the engine. She doesn't lock the doors.

She stares at the curve in the headlights: the guardrail, the dark trees, the ravine below.

Her hand drifts to the door handle.

Rests there.

ARIANNA
(whispering into the phone)
He was walking home, Marc.
(beat)
He was just walking home.

HOLD on her face in the dashboard glow, her hand on the door handle, the curve waiting in the light.

FADE OUT.

> END OF EPISODE #0005 <

===

> NEXT ON BEYOND THE KINGDOM... <

!— On Northside Drive, MARC pulls ARIANNA into his arms by the guardrail: "Look at me. I will handle this. I've always handled this."

!— In a Buckhead parking deck, AMOND offers HARMONY the full resources of BSI to find her brother. HARMONY SLAPS him: "You think money fixes everything?"

!— In the Belle Rive laundry room, NATASHA lifts ROBERT's dress shirt from the hamper and holds the collar to her face. An unfamiliar perfume. She knows exactly whose it is. It's called Smile.

> FADE TO BLACK. <
